  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Yes. The Manual says that a change of ownership of a registered trade mark, or one whose registration is being sought, must be notified to the Registrar as soon as it occurs; transfer to an estate should therefore be treated as an ownership-recordal matter.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part43/1#1~1",
          "quote": "Assignment or transmission of a trade mark refers to the process by which ownership of a registered trade mark or a trade mark whose registration is being sought may be passed from one party to another. The Act requires that the Registrar be advised of this change of ownership as soon as it occurs."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual distinguishes assignment by act of the parties from transmission, which includes devolution on a deceased person’s personal representative; the appropriate description depends on how ownership passes.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part43/1#1~1",
          "quote": "1.1 The Act defines assignment in relation to trade marks as meaning assignment by the act of the parties concerned. 1.2 It defines transmission as transmission by operation of law, or devolution on the personal representative of a deceased person, or any other kind of transfer except assignment (section 6)."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under the Manual’s recordal guidance, the Registrar must record the ownership change if the application meets the formal requirements and is supported by a document establishing title.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part43/1#1~1",
          "quote": "If the application to record complies with the formal requirements and is supported by a document that establishes title to the trade mark, the Registrar must record the change of ownership."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For a trade mark application, section 108 requires recordal and publication of a complying assignment or transmission, and the transferee becomes the applicant from the day of recordal.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s108",
          "quote": "(1) If the application complies with this Act, the Registrar must: (a) at, or within, the time provided for in the regulations, record in the manner that the Registrar thinks fit (but not in the Register) the particulars of the assignment or transmission; and (b) publish the particulars of the assignment or transmission in accordance with the regulations. (2) On and after the day on which the Registrar records the particulars of the assignment or transmission, the person to whom the trade mark has been assigned or transmitted is taken, for the purposes of this Act, to be the applicant for the registration of the trade mark."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Separately, the estate’s ongoing control of the name or image remains relevant under the Manual’s section 43 practice, notwithstanding its general position that long-deceased historical figures ordinarily do not give rise to that ground.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/6/6/2~1",
          "quote": "Generally, a section 43 ground for rejection will not arise in respect of long deceased historical figures, even if they were well known for the same field as the specified goods/services are in."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/6/6/2~1",
          "quote": "However, a relevant connotation may arise if the deceased person’s estate (or other authorised entity) has continued to control the use of the person’s name/image after their death, e.g. by commercialising directly or licensing to third parties."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual says a section 43 ground may be warranted where research indicates continued use or control, awareness of that association among a significant number or sector of relevant Australian consumers, and a connection with the specified goods or services.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part29/6/6/2~1",
          "quote": "A section 43 ground for rejection may therefore be warranted, having regard to the considerations at Part 29.6 above, if research indicates that: the deceased person’s estate (or other authorised entity) has continued to use, or control the use of, the person’s name/image after their death; a significant number or a significant sector of relevant Australian consumers would know that use of the deceased person’s name/image is associated with their estate (or other authorised entity); and there is a connection between the name/image of the deceased person and the specified goods/services."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The statutory question under section 43 is whether a connotation makes use of the trade mark for the particular goods or services likely to deceive or cause confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s43",
          "quote": "An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "The supplied passages do not establish that recording ownership in the estate automatically resolves the separate section 43 question.",
      "quotes": [],
      "authority": "none"
    }
  ],
